--------------080103020002090105030209
Content-Type: text/plain;
	format=flowed;
	charset=ISO-8859-1
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit

Hi JJ,

sorry it took me so long to pass on the routines that calculate the 
access times of
sky locations as seen from a satellite in low earth orbit.

For the way we do it the key is a file that  'most.type.orbit.sav' 
(which is part
of the zip file that is attached) which lists the apparent positions 
(RA,DEC) of the
earth as seen from the satellite over time. A full year is covered with 
a two minutes
resolution after which the positions in first order repeat. If other 
orbits need to be
investigated such a file has to be constructed.
We use STK (Satellite Tool Kit) a commercial software for which one can 
get a
free academic license, to calculate the position information. In fact if 
one wants
a very precise analysis of satellite access times down to the operator 
level this
STK is the software to use.

You will also find three IDL programs:

satskyaccess.pro
which is an example how to read the 'most.type.orbit.sav' file and 
process the
information so that you get the times when a sky position can be seen by the
satellite ie when the sun or the earth is off your limiting angles.

You can modify this as you see fit for your analysis!!!!

There are two routines that are called in that program which are  in the
IDL Astronomy Users Library at:  
http://idlastro.gsfc.nasa.gov/homepage.html
'sunpos.pro' calculated the Sun's RA and DEC as a function of time (JD)
and 'sphdist.pro' which calculates the angular distance between points 
on a sphere.

Both of the those routines call other utility programs which you can 
retrieve from the
IDL library see above link.

I hope that helps you get started with your investigation.

Cheers and Happy New Year!
Rainer



